\Method{\EXP{\KWD{opr} (\KWD{self})! \mathrel{\mathtt{:}} \mathbb{Z}}}

The factorial operator is defined only for natural number types;
it returns the product of all positive integers that are not less than the integer.
If the argument is \EXP{0}, the result is \EXP{1}.

%%   property 0! = 1
%%   property FORALL (m) m >= 0 IMPLIES (m+1)! = (m+1) m!
\begin{Fortress}
{\tt~~}\pushtabs\=\+\(  \KWD{property} 0! = 1\)\\
\(  \KWD{property} \forall (m)\; m \geq 0 \rightarrow (m+1)! = (m+1)\:m!\)\-\\\poptabs
\end{Fortress}
\revision{revival-range-examples}{The Working Draft of February 2011 stated
this property as \EXP{m! = \prod\limits_{k\leftarrow{}1\COLONOP{}m} k}, a
product over a range of \EXP{\mathbb{Z}} values, which \secref{ranges} no
longer admits; the two properties above state it by the value at \EXP{0} and a
recurrence, for every natural \VAR{m}.}


